[36m(TaskRunner pid=122367)[0m This means the number $d$ must end in the digit 1. Such numbers are odd and not divisible by 5. Therefore, any divisor $d$ that ends in 1 must not be divisible by 2 or 5. That implies that the prime factorization of $d$ must only include the primes in the factorization of $9!$ that are not 2 or 5.
